In C#, the CloseReason property of the FormClosingEventArgs class can be used to determine the reason for a form closure. The possible CloseReason values include:

1. CloseReason.None: No reason specified or the form was closed programmatically.
2. CloseReason.UserClosing: The user closed the form by clicking the close button.
3. CloseReason.MdiFormClosing: The parent form is closing an MDI child form.
4. CloseReason.FormOwnerClosing: The owner form is closing a owned form.
5. CloseReason.ApplicationExitCall: The Application.Exit() method was called to exit the application.
6. CloseReason.TaskManagerClosing: The user ends the application using the Task Manager (Task Manager will display "End Task").
7. CloseReason.WindowsShutDown: The operating system is shutting down.

To specifically handle the case when the application is closed using the Task Manager or when it is not responding, you can check for the CloseReason.TaskManagerClosing value. Here's an example:

```csharp
private void Form1_FormClosing(object sender, FormClosingEventArgs e)
{
    if (e.CloseReason == CloseReason.TaskManagerClosing)
    {
        // Application is ending through Task Manager
        // Handle the necessary cleanup or logging here
    }
    else if (e.CloseReason == CloseReason.WindowsShutDown)
    {
        // Application is ending due to Windows shutdown
        // Handle the necessary cleanup or logging here
    }
    // Other close reasons can be handled similarly
}
```

By checking the CloseReason value, you can handle different scenarios accordingly and perform any necessary cleanup or logging tasks before the application exits.

