To remove the title bar and keep the borders in a C# WinForms application, you can modify the Form's properties and handle the form's events. 

First, set the `FormBorderStyle` property to specify the type of border, such as Sizable, Fixed3D, FixedSingle, etc. Then, set the `ControlBox` property to `false` to hide the standard title bar buttons (minimize, maximize, close). 

In addition, override the `WndProc` method and handle the `WM_NCHITTEST` message to allow the user to move the form by clicking and dragging its border.

Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Windows.Forms;

public partial class MainForm : Form
{
    public MainForm()
    {
        InitializeComponent();

        // Set form properties
        this.FormBorderStyle = FormBorderStyle.FixedSingle;
        this.ControlBox = false;
    }

    // Override WndProc to handle WM_NCHITTEST message 
    protected override void WndProc(ref Message m)
    {
        const int WM_NCHITTEST = 0x84;
        const int HT_CAPTION = 0x2;

        if (m.Msg == WM_NCHITTEST)
        {
            // Allow moving the form by clicking and dragging its border
            base.WndProc(ref m);
            if ((int)m.Result == HT_CAPTION)
                m.Result = (IntPtr)HT_CLIENT;
        }
        else
        {
            base.WndProc(ref m);
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, the `WM_NCHITTEST` constant represents the message code for determining what part of the window is at a specific point. The `HT_CAPTION` value specifies that the point is on the title bar. By changing the result value to `HT_CLIENT`, the window will be movable when the user clicks and drags anywhere on the form's border.

Make sure to replace `MainForm` with the name of your actual form class.

