The jittering issue with the camera could be due to the frame rate dropping on lower-end PCs or when the player character moves faster than just walking. Lower frame rates can result in stuttering or jittering camera movements.

To address this issue, you can try implementing interpolation or smoothing techniques to make the camera movement appear smoother even at lower frame rates. Here are a few suggestions:

1. Time.deltaTime: Multiply the input values (x and y) by Time.deltaTime. This will make the camera movement frame-rate independent and smooth out the jittering effect. You can modify the "GetInputs()" method as follows:

```csharp
private void GetInputs()
{
    // if (Input.GetMouseButton(1))
    // {
        x += Input.GetAxis("Mouse X") * xSpeed * sensitivity * Time.deltaTime;
        y -= Input.GetAxis("Mouse Y") * ySpeed * sensitivity * Time.deltaTime;

        y = Mathf.Clamp(y, yMinLimit, yMaxLimit);
    // }

    distance = Mathf.Clamp(distance - Input.GetAxis("Mouse ScrollWheel") * distance, distanceMin, distanceMax);
}
```

2. Camera smoothing: Instead of directly assigning the rotation values (xSmooth, ySmooth) to the camera's local rotation, you can gradually interpolate towards the target rotation using Quaternion.Lerp or Quaternion.Slerp. This can help in smoothing out sudden camera movements. Modify the "MoveCamera()" method as follows:

```csharp
private void MoveCamera()
{
    xSmooth = Mathf.SmoothDamp(xSmooth, x, ref xVelocity, smoothTime);
    ySmooth = Mathf.SmoothDamp(ySmooth, y, ref yVelocity, smoothTime);
    distanceSmooth = Mathf.SmoothDamp(distanceSmooth, distance, ref distanceVelocity, smoothTime);

    Quaternion targetRotation = Quaternion.Euler(ySmooth, xSmooth, 0);
    transform.localRotation = Quaternion.Lerp(transform.localRotation, targetRotation, smoothTime * Time.deltaTime);

    Vector3 desiredPosition = transform.rotation * new Vector3(0.0f, 0.0f, -distanceSmooth) + target.position;
    Vector3 collisionFreePosition = CheckCollision(target.position, desiredPosition);
    transform.position = collisionFreePosition;
}
```

3. Adjusting smoothTime: You can experiment with different values for the "smoothTime" variable to find the right balance between smoothness and responsiveness. A smaller value might make the camera feel more responsive but could lead to more jittering, while a larger value will make the camera movement appear smoother but less responsive.

Try implementing these changes and see if the camera jittering issue improves. Additionally, optimizing other aspects of your game, such as reducing the number of physics calculations or simplifying collision detection, can also help improve performance on lower-end PCs.

