To get the physical address from a `dmabuf` file descriptor in Linux, you can use the `ioctl` system call with the `DMA_BUF_IOCTL_PHYS` command. Here's an example of how to do it:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/ioctl.h>
#include <linux/dma-buf.h>

int main() {
    int dmabuf_fd = open("/dev/dma-buf", O_RDWR);  // Replace with the actual path to your dmabuf file

    unsigned long phys_addr;
    if (ioctl(dmabuf_fd, DMA_BUF_IOCTL_PHYS, &phys_addr) == -1) {
        perror("ioctl");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Physical Address: %lx\n", phys_addr);

    close(dmabuf_fd);

    return 0;
}
```

Make sure to replace `"/dev/dma-buf"` with the path to your `dmabuf` file. The `DMA_BUF_IOCTL_PHYS` command is used to retrieve the physical address from the `dmabuf` file descriptor, and it fills the `phys_addr` variable with the result.

Compile the code by running `gcc your_program.c -o your_program`, and then execute the program. It should output the physical address of the `dmabuf` file descriptor.

Note: The `DMA_BUF_IOCTL_PHYS` command is specific to the Linux kernel implementation of `dmabuf`. If you are working in a different environment or on a different operating system, the method may vary.

