To detect if both Left Alt and Return keys are pressed using SDL2 in C++, you can use the following approach:

```cpp
#include <SDL2/SDL.h>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    // Initialize SDL library
    if (SDL_Init(SDL_INIT_VIDEO) != 0) {
        std::cout << "Error initializing SDL: " << SDL_GetError() << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    // Create a window
    SDL_Window* window = SDL_CreateWindow("Key Event Example", SDL_WINDOWPOS_UNDEFINED, SDL_WINDOWPOS_UNDEFINED, 800, 600, SDL_WINDOW_SHOWN);
    if (window == nullptr) {
        std::cout << "Error creating window: " << SDL_GetError() << std::endl;
        SDL_Quit();
        return 1;
    }

    // Create a renderer for the window
    SDL_Renderer* renderer = SDL_CreateRenderer(window, -1, SDL_RENDERER_ACCELERATED);
    if (renderer == nullptr) {
        std::cout << "Error creating renderer: " << SDL_GetError() << std::endl;
        SDL_DestroyWindow(window);
        SDL_Quit();
        return 1;
    }

    bool quit = false;
    SDL_Event event;
    bool leftAltPressed = false;
    bool returnPressed = false;

    while (!quit) {
        while (SDL_PollEvent(&event)) {
            if (event.type == SDL_QUIT) {
                quit = true;
                break;
            }
            if (event.type == SDL_KEYDOWN) {
                if (event.key.keysym.sym == SDLK_LALT) {
                    leftAltPressed = true;
                }
                if (event.key.keysym.sym == SDLK_RETURN) {
                    returnPressed = true;
                }
                if (leftAltPressed && returnPressed) {
                    std::cout << "Both Left Alt and Return keys are pressed!" << std::endl;
                }
            }
            if (event.type == SDL_KEYUP) {
                if (event.key.keysym.sym == SDLK_LALT) {
                    leftAltPressed = false;
                }
                if (event.key.keysym.sym == SDLK_RETURN) {
                    returnPressed = false;
                }
            }
        }
        SDL_RenderPresent(renderer);
    }

    SDL_DestroyRenderer(renderer);
    SDL_DestroyWindow(window);
    SDL_Quit();

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, we set `leftAltPressed` and `returnPressed` flags to track whether the Left Alt and Return keys are currently pressed or not. When a keydown event occurs, we check if the keys correspond to Left Alt and Return keys using the `SDLK_LALT` and `SDLK_RETURN` key codes. If both flags are true, we print the desired message. Similarly, when a keyup event occurs, we reset the flags accordingly.

The `SDL_Event` structure in SDL can only carry a single event at a time. Each time you call `SDL_PollEvent()` or `SDL_WaitEvent()`, it fetches one event from the event queue and stores it in the `SDL_Event` structure for further processing.

However, it is important to note that the event queue can hold multiple events. By continuously calling `SDL_PollEvent()` or `SDL_WaitEvent()` in a loop, you can process and handle multiple events in succession.

Here's an example of how you can process multiple events using `SDL_PollEvent()`:

```cpp
SDL_Event event;
while (SDL_PollEvent(&event)) {
    if (event.type == SDL_QUIT) {
        // Handle quit event
    } else if (event.type == SDL_KEYDOWN) {
        // Handle key press event
    } else if (event.type == SDL_MOUSEBUTTONDOWN) {
        // Handle mouse button press event
    }
    // Additional event handling code...
}
```

In this example, the loop continuously calls `SDL_PollEvent()` to retrieve events from the queue and stores them in the `SDL_Event` structure. It then checks the `type` field of the structure to determine the type of event and handles it accordingly. Additional event handling code can be added as needed within the loop.

Remember, each iteration of the loop processes one event from the queue, so if there are multiple events waiting in the queue, they will be processed one after another in subsequent iterations of the loop.

The choice between using a series of if statements or if-else statements when polling SDL events depends on the specific requirements and logic of your application. Both methods can effectively handle events, so you can choose the approach that best suits your needs and coding style.

Using a series of if statements allows you to handle each event separately and independently. This approach is useful when you want to process different events in different ways or if you need to check multiple conditions for each event.

```cpp
SDL_Event event;
while (SDL_PollEvent(&event)) {
    if (event.type == SDL_QUIT) {
        // Handle quit event
    }

    if (event.type == SDL_KEYDOWN) {
        // Handle key press event
    }

    if (event.type == SDL_MOUSEBUTTONDOWN) {
        // Handle mouse button press event
    }

    // Additional event handling code...
}
```

On the other hand, using if-else statements can be beneficial if you have a series of mutually exclusive conditions and you want to execute only one block of code based on the first matching condition. This approach can streamline your code, especially if you have a large number of different events with specific actions.

```cpp
SDL_Event event;
while (SDL_PollEvent(&event)) {
    if (event.type == SDL_QUIT) {
        // Handle quit event
    } else if (event.type == SDL_KEYDOWN) {
        // Handle key press event
    } else if (event.type == SDL_MOUSEBUTTONDOWN) {
        // Handle mouse button press event
    }

    // Additional event handling code...
}
```

Ultimately, the choice between using if statements or if-else statements depends on the specific event handling requirements of your application and the readability/maintainability preferences of your coding style.

