When one circle is located at the intersection of two grid cells, the challenge is to determine which grid cell(s) the circle is actually present in. Here's an example of how you can handle this case using C#:

First, let's assume that each grid cell has a size of `cellSize` and the coordinates of the circle's center are `(circleX, circleY)`.

```csharp
public class Grid
{
    private readonly int cellSize;
    private readonly Dictionary<int, List<Circle>> gridCells;

    public Grid(int cellSize)
    {
        this.cellSize = cellSize;
        gridCells = new Dictionary<int, List<Circle>>();
    }

    public void AddCircle(Circle circle)
    {
        // Calculate the grid cell indices based on circle's position
        int cellX1 = Math.Floor(circle.X / cellSize);
        int cellY1 = Math.Floor(circle.Y / cellSize);
        int cellX2 = Math.Floor((circle.X + circle.Radius) / cellSize);
        int cellY2 = Math.Floor((circle.Y + circle.Radius) / cellSize);

        // Add the circle to the relevant grid cells (including intersection cells)
        for (int x = cellX1; x <= cellX2; x++)
        {
            for (int y = cellY1; y <= cellY2; y++)
            {
                int cellIndex = CalculateCellIndex(x, y);
                if (!gridCells.ContainsKey(cellIndex))
                    gridCells[cellIndex] = new List<Circle>();
                gridCells[cellIndex].Add(circle);
            }
        }
    }

    public List<Circle> GetCollisions(Circle circle)
    {
        // Calculate the grid cell indices based on circle's position
        int cellX1 = Math.Floor(circle.X / cellSize);
        int cellY1 = Math.Floor(circle.Y / cellSize);
        int cellX2 = Math.Floor((circle.X + circle.Radius) / cellSize);
        int cellY2 = Math.Floor((circle.Y + circle.Radius) / cellSize);

        List<Circle> collisions = new List<Circle>();

        // Check for collisions in the relevant grid cells (including intersection cells)
        for (int x = cellX1; x <= cellX2; x++)
        {
            for (int y = cellY1; y <= cellY2; y++)
            {
                int cellIndex = CalculateCellIndex(x, y);
                if (gridCells.TryGetValue(cellIndex, out List<Circle> circlesInCell))
                {
                    foreach (var otherCircle in circlesInCell)
                    {
                        if (circle.Intersects(otherCircle))  // Assuming Circle.Intersects method exists
                        {
                            collisions.Add(otherCircle);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return collisions;
    }

    private int CalculateCellIndex(int x, int y)
    {
        // Calculate a unique index for the grid cell based on its coordinates
        return x + y * 1000;  // Assuming grid size is 1000x1000
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a `Grid` class that maintains a dictionary `gridCells`, where each key is a unique index representing a grid cell and the value is a list of circles present in that grid cell.

To handle the case of one circle located at the intersection of two grid cells, we iterate over all the grid cells from `(cellX1, cellY1)` to `(cellX2, cellY2)`. This ensures that we consider all the grid cells that may intersect with the circle. We add the circle to all the relevant grid cells, even if it's on the intersection, allowing proper collision detection.

When checking for collisions using the `GetCollisions` method, we use the same approach to iterate over the relevant grid cells and check for intersection with other circles. Finally, we return the list of collided circles.

Note: The `Circle` class and its `Intersects` method are assumed to be implemented separately according to your needs.

