To list files in a folder on Windows using C++, you can use the Windows API and the FindFirstFile and FindNextFile functions. Here is an example code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <Windows.h>

std::vector<std::string> listFilesInFolder(const std::string& folderPath) {
    std::vector<std::string> fileNames;
    WIN32_FIND_DATAA findData;
    HANDLE hFind = INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE;

    // Construct the search path
    std::string searchPath = folderPath + "\\*";

    // Find the first file in the directory
    hFind = FindFirstFileA(searchPath.c_str(), &findData);

    if (hFind != INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE) {
        do {
            // Exclude directories
            if ((findData.dwFileAttributes & FILE_ATTRIBUTE_DIRECTORY) == 0) {
                fileNames.push_back(findData.cFileName);
            }
        } while (FindNextFileA(hFind, &findData) != 0);

        // Close the directory handle
        FindClose(hFind);
    } else {
        std::cout << "Error opening directory." << std::endl;
        return fileNames;
    }

    return fileNames;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> files = listFilesInFolder("C:\\path\\to\\folder");

    // Print the file names
    for (const std::string& fileName : files) {
        std::cout << fileName << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, the `listFilesInFolder` function takes a folder path as input and returns a vector of file names in that folder. It uses the `FindFirstFileA` and `FindNextFileA` functions to iterate through the files in the folder. It excludes directories using the `FILE_ATTRIBUTE_DIRECTORY` flag.

In the `main` function, you can call `listFilesInFolder` with the desired folder path and print the file names using a loop.

