To find the mapped memory addresses of a process in C#, you can use the `Process` class from the `System.Diagnostics` namespace along with the `ProcessModule` class. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Diagnostics;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Enter process name:");
        string processName = Console.ReadLine();

        Process process = Process.GetProcessesByName(processName)[0];

        foreach (ProcessModule module in process.Modules)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Module name: {0}", module.ModuleName);
            Console.WriteLine("Base address: 0x{0}", module.BaseAddress.ToString("X"));
            Console.WriteLine();
        }

        Console.ReadLine();
    }
}
```

In this example, the program prompts the user to enter the name of the process they want to find the mapped memory addresses for. It then uses `Process.GetProcessesByName` to get the `Process` object representing that process. The `Modules` property of the `Process` object provides a collection of `ProcessModule` objects, each representing a module (DLL or EXE) loaded into the process.

The program then iterates over each module and prints its name and base address in hexadecimal format.

Note that this example assumes that the process name entered by the user refers to a process that is currently running. If the process is not found or the user inputs an incorrect process name, this code will throw an exception. You can handle such cases by adding appropriate error handling code.

