The ViewHolder pattern is used in Android to optimize the performance of RecyclerViews and ListViews by reducing the number of calls to findViewById(). It involves creating a ViewHolder class that holds references to the views in each item of the list, which can then be reused as needed.

Here is an example of generating code for an ArrayAdapter using the ViewHolder pattern:

```java
public class MyAdapter extends ArrayAdapter<String> {

    private static class ViewHolder {
        TextView textView;
        // Other views in the item layout
    }

    public MyAdapter(Context context, List<String> items) {
        super(context, 0, items);
    }

    @Override
    public View getView(int position, View convertView, ViewGroup parent) {
        ViewHolder viewHolder;

        if (convertView == null) {
            convertView = LayoutInflater.from(getContext()).inflate(R.layout.item_layout, parent, false);

            viewHolder = new ViewHolder();
            viewHolder.textView = convertView.findViewById(R.id.text_view);
            // Initialize other views in the item layout and assign them to the viewHolder

            convertView.setTag(viewHolder);
        } else {
            viewHolder = (ViewHolder) convertView.getTag();
        }

        String item = getItem(position);

        if (item != null) {
            viewHolder.textView.setText(item);
            // Set values for other views in the item layout using viewHolder
        }

        return convertView;
    }
}
```

In this example, the ViewHolder class holds a reference to the TextView in each item of the list. In the getView() method, if convertView is null, it inflates the item layout and initializes the ViewHolder's views. The ViewHolder object is then set as a tag on the convertView. If convertView is not null, it retrieves the ViewHolder from the convertView's tag.

This pattern helps to avoid the frequent and expensive calls to findViewById() by recycling the views that have already been inflated and initialized, resulting in improved performance.

If you have multiple LinearLayouts in your ArrayAdapter, you can create multiple ViewHolder classes to hold references to the views in each LinearLayout. Alternatively, you can create separate Adapters for each LinearLayout if they have significantly different layouts or behavior.

Here is an example of using multiple ViewHolder classes for multiple LinearLayouts in an ArrayAdapter:

```java
public class MyAdapter extends ArrayAdapter<String> {

    private static class ViewHolder1 {
        TextView textView;
        // Other views in LinearLayout 1
    }

    private static class ViewHolder2 {
        TextView textView;
        // Other views in LinearLayout 2
    }

    public MyAdapter(Context context, List<String> items) {
        super(context, 0, items);
    }

    @Override
    public int getItemViewType(int position) {
        // Determine the type of layout for the item at position
        // Return different values for different types of LinearLayouts
    }

    @Override
    public int getViewTypeCount() {
        // Return the total number of different types of LinearLayouts
    }

    @Override
    public View getView(int position, View convertView, ViewGroup parent) {
        int viewType = getItemViewType(position);

        if (convertView == null) {
            LayoutInflater inflater = LayoutInflater.from(getContext());

            switch (viewType) {
                case 0:
                    convertView = inflater.inflate(R.layout.layout_1, parent, false);
                    ViewHolder1 viewHolder1 = new ViewHolder1();
                    viewHolder1.textView = convertView.findViewById(R.id.text_view_1);
                    // Initialize other views in LinearLayout 1 and assign them to viewHolder1
                    convertView.setTag(viewHolder1);
                    break;
                case 1:
                    convertView = inflater.inflate(R.layout.layout_2, parent, false);
                    ViewHolder2 viewHolder2 = new ViewHolder2();
                    viewHolder2.textView = convertView.findViewById(R.id.text_view_2);
                    // Initialize other views in LinearLayout 2 and assign them to viewHolder2
                    convertView.setTag(viewHolder2);
                    break;
                // Create and initialize other ViewHolder classes for other types of LinearLayouts
            }
        }

        Object viewHolderObject = convertView.getTag();

        switch (viewType) {
            case 0:
                ViewHolder1 viewHolder1 = (ViewHolder1) viewHolderObject;
                String item1 = getItem(position);
                if (item1 != null) {
                    viewHolder1.textView.setText(item1);
                    // Set values for other views in LinearLayout 1 using viewHolder1
                }
                break;
            case 1:
                ViewHolder2 viewHolder2 = (ViewHolder2) viewHolderObject;
                String item2 = getItem(position);
                if (item2 != null) {
                    viewHolder2.textView.setText(item2);
                    // Set values for other views in LinearLayout 2 using viewHolder2
                }
                break;
            // Access and update views in other types of LinearLayouts using respective ViewHolder classes
        }

        return convertView;
    }
}
```

In this example, getItemViewType() method is overridden to determine the type of layout for each item based on its position. getViewTypeCount() method returns the total number of different types of LinearLayouts. Depending on the type of layout, a different ViewHolder is created and initialized in the getView() method. The tag of convertView is set to the respective ViewHolder object.

Then, in the getView() method, the viewHolderObject is retrieved based on the layout type, and the respective ViewHolder class is identified. The values for the views in each LinearLayout are set using the appropriate ViewHolder object.

This approach allows you to handle multiple LinearLayouts with different layouts and behavior in a single ArrayAdapter. However, if the LinearLayouts have significantly different layouts or behavior, it may be more maintainable to create separate Adapters for each LinearLayout.

Given an array of random integers, Push all the zero’s of a given array to the end of the array. For example, an array before and after reorganization is like:
Before: arr[] = 1, 2, 0, 4, 3, 0, 5, 0;
After: arr[] = 1, 2, 4, 3, 5, 0, 0, 0;
Write in Java or pseudo-code. You are recommended to use the following pseudo-code style.

Here is the pseudo-code to push all the zeroes to the end of the array:

1. Initialize two variables, i and j, with the value 0.
2. Iterate through the input array from left to right using a for loop with index variable k:
   a. If the value at arr[k] is not equal to zero, then swap the value at arr[k] with the value at arr[i].
   b. Increment the value of i by 1.
3. Finally, iterate through the input array from left to right using another for loop with index variable k:
   a. Assign the value 0 to arr[k] for any remaining indices.
4. The modified array will have all the zeroes pushed to the end.

The Java code implementation for the above pseudo-code is as follows:

```java
public class ZeroesToEnd {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = { 1, 2, 0, 4, 3, 0, 5, 0 };
        pushZeroesToEnd(arr);
        for (int num : arr) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }

    public static void pushZeroesToEnd(int[] arr) {
        int i = 0;
        for (int k = 0; k < arr.length; k++) {
            if (arr[k] != 0) {
                int temp = arr[k];
                arr[k] = arr[i];
                arr[i] = temp;
                i++;
            }
        }
        for (int k = i; k < arr.length; k++) {
            arr[k] = 0;
        }
    }
}
