```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class SquareProgram {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Read an integer from the user
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter the size of the square: ");
        int size = scanner.nextInt();

        // Call the method to display the filled and hollow square
        displaySquare(size);
    }

    public static void displaySquare(int size) {
        // Display the filled square
        System.out.println("Filled Square:");
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < size; j++) {
                System.out.print("* ");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }

        // Display an empty line
        System.out.println();

        // Display the hollow square
        System.out.println("Hollow Square:");
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < size; j++) {
                if (i == 0 || i == size - 1 || j == 0 || j == size - 1) {
                    System.out.print("* ");
                } else {
                    System.out.print("  ");
                }
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

In this program, we first read an integer from the user to determine the size of the square. Then, we call the `displaySquare` method to display the filled and hollow square using asterisks.

The `displaySquare` method consists of two nested loops. The outer loop iterates `size` times to handle each row, and the inner loop iterates `size` times to handle each column. 

For the filled square, we simply print an asterisk followed by a space for each position.

For the hollow square, we check if the current position is on the boundary (first row, last row, first column, or last column). If it is, we print an asterisk followed by a space; otherwise, we print two spaces to create the hollow effect.

