Here is a Java implementation to reverse a queue using another queue:

```java
import java.util.Queue;
import java.util.LinkedList;

public class ReverseQueue {
    public static Queue<Integer> reverse(Queue<Integer> originalQueue) {
        Queue<Integer> reversedQueue = new LinkedList<Integer>();

        while (!originalQueue.isEmpty()) {
            reversedQueue.add(originalQueue.poll());
        }

        return reversedQueue;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Queue<Integer> originalQueue = new LinkedList<Integer>();
        originalQueue.add(1);
        originalQueue.add(2);
        originalQueue.add(3);
        originalQueue.add(4);
        originalQueue.add(5);

        Queue<Integer> reversedQueue = reverse(originalQueue);

        while (!reversedQueue.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.print(reversedQueue.poll() + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

The output will be: 5 4 3 2 1

The algorithm works by iterating through the original queue and removing each element from it using `poll()`. The removed element is then added to the reversed queue using `add()`. Finally, the reversed queue is returned.

