To disable Bluetooth when the app is closed in Android API 33 using Android Studio, you can follow these steps:

1. First, add the necessary permissions to your app's Manifest file. Open the AndroidManifest.xml file and add the following line inside the <manifest> tag:

```xml
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH" />
```

2. Create a new class that extends the Application class. This class will be responsible for monitoring the app's state and handling the Bluetooth disabling logic. For example, create a BluetoothApplication class:

```java
public class BluetoothApplication extends Application {

    @Override
    public void onCreate() {
        super.onCreate();

        // Register a broadcast receiver to listen for app close events
        registerReceiver(new BroadcastReceiver() {
            @Override
            public void onReceive(Context context, Intent intent) {
                // Check if the app is being closed
                if (intent.getAction().equals(Intent.ACTION_CLOSE_SYSTEM_DIALOGS)) {
                    // Disable Bluetooth
                    BluetoothAdapter bluetoothAdapter = BluetoothAdapter.getDefaultAdapter();
                    if (bluetoothAdapter.isEnabled()) {
                        bluetoothAdapter.disable();
                    }
                }
            }
        }, new IntentFilter(Intent.ACTION_CLOSE_SYSTEM_DIALOGS));
    }
}
```

3. Open the AndroidManifest.xml file again and add the android:name attribute to the <application> tag, specifying the BluetoothApplication class:

```xml
<application
    android:name=".BluetoothApplication"
    ...
```

4. Build and run your application on a device with Bluetooth capability.

Now, whenever the app is closed by the user, the broadcast receiver will detect it and disable Bluetooth if it's enabled.

