To display all elements of the inner lists in the outer list, you need to adjust the `onMeasure()` method of the child `ListView` within the custom adapter's `getView()` method. 

Update the `getView()` method in the `CustomAdapter` class as follows:

```java
@Override
public View getView(int position, View convertView, ViewGroup parent) {
    ViewHolder holder;

    if (convertView == null) {
        convertView = inflater.inflate(R.layout.row_item, parent, false);

        holder = new ViewHolder();
        holder.childListView = convertView.findViewById(R.id.childListView);
        convertView.setTag(holder);
    } else {
        holder = (ViewHolder) convertView.getTag();
    }

    // Bind child data to the child ListView
    ChildAdapter childAdapter = new ChildAdapter(context, parentList.get(position));
    holder.childListView.setAdapter(childAdapter);

    // Adjust the height of the child ListView
    int totalHeight = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < childAdapter.getCount(); i++) {
        View listItem = childAdapter.getView(i, null, holder.childListView);
        listItem.measure(0, 0);
        totalHeight += listItem.getMeasuredHeight();
    }

    ViewGroup.LayoutParams params = holder.childListView.getLayoutParams();
    params.height = totalHeight + (holder.childListView.getDividerHeight() * (childAdapter.getCount() - 1));
    holder.childListView.setLayoutParams(params);
    holder.childListView.requestLayout();

    return convertView;
}
```

With this modification, the height of the child `ListView` will adjust dynamically based on the number of elements in each inner list, allowing all elements to be displayed properly.

