The `@interface` keyword in Java is used to define an annotation type. Annotations are a way to attach metadata or additional information to Java code elements such as classes, methods, fields, and parameters.

Here is an example of defining a custom annotation type using the `@interface` keyword:

```java
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)  // Specifies that the annotation should be retained at runtime
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)  // Specifies that the annotation can only be applied to classes
public @interface CustomAnnotation {
    String value() default "";  // Define an element named "value" with a default value of an empty string
    int count() default 0;  // Define an element named "count" with a default value of 0
}
```

In this example, we define a custom annotation type called `CustomAnnotation`. The annotation can be applied to classes using `@CustomAnnotation`.

The annotation has two elements, `value()` and `count()`, which can be used to assign values to the annotation when it is applied.

To use this custom annotation, you can apply it to a class like this:

```java
@CustomAnnotation(value = "MyAnnotation", count = 5)
public class MyClass {
    // Class implementation
}
```

By applying the annotation to `MyClass`, you can retrieve the annotation and its values at runtime using reflection.

